    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old postmenopausal woman is diagnosed with osteoporosis and started on alendronate. She asks about calcium and vitamin D supplementation. Her dietary calcium intake is approximately 400 mg/day. Her 25-hydroxyvitamin D level is 23 ng/mL. Which of the following is the most appropriate supplementation recommendation?', '["Total daily calcium intake (diet + supplements) should be 1200 mg/day for women > 50; vitamin D intake should be 800-1000 IU/day to maintain 25(OH)D levels >= 30 ng/mL; take calcium in divided doses (no more than 500-600 mg per dose) with food for optimal absorption", "Calcium supplementation of 2500 mg/day is recommended for all patients with osteoporosis to maximize bone density", "Vitamin D supplementation is unnecessary if the patient takes calcium supplements, because calcium is absorbed independently of vitamin D", "There is no upper limit for calcium supplementation; higher doses always lead to greater bone density gains", "Calcium carbonate should always be taken on an empty stomach for best absorption"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **NOF and Endocrine Society** recommend a **total daily calcium intake of 1200 mg/day** for women over 50 and men over 70 (from diet + supplements combined). Key points: (1) **Calcium should be taken in divided doses** (no more than 500-600 mg per dose) because fractional absorption decreases with higher single doses. (2) **Calcium carbonate** requires gastric acid for absorption and should be taken **with meals**. **Calcium citrate** can be taken with or without food and is preferred for patients on PPIs or with achlorhydria. (3) **Vitamin D** (800-1000 IU/day, or more to correct deficiency) is essential to maintain **25(OH)D levels >= 30 ng/mL** for optimal calcium absorption and musculoskeletal health. (4) Excessive calcium supplementation (>2000-2500 mg/day) increases the risk of **nephrolithiasis** and possibly **cardiovascular events** and should be avoided. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Endocrine Society Guidelines)'),
