ard''s Ch. 77; Endocrine Society Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old postmenopausal woman with osteopenia (T-score -2.0) asks what exercises she should perform to help prevent osteoporosis and fractures. She has no history of fractures and is in good general health. Which of the following exercise recommendations is most appropriate?', '["Only flexibility exercises such as yoga and stretching are sufficient for osteoporosis prevention", "Bed rest and complete avoidance of physical activity is recommended to prevent fractures in patients with low bone density", "Weight-bearing aerobic exercises (e.g., brisk walking, jogging, stair climbing, dancing) and resistance/strength training are recommended to maintain bone density, improve muscle strength, and reduce fall risk; high-impact activities should be individualized based on fracture risk", "Vigorous high-impact activities (e.g., heavy jumping, plyometrics) are recommended for all elderly patients regardless of bone density or fracture risk", "Only swimming and aquatic exercises are recommended for osteoporosis prevention because they reduce impact on bones"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Weight-bearing and resistance exercises** are the most effective non-pharmacologic interventions for maintaining and improving bone density: (1) **Weight-bearing aerobic exercises** (walking, jogging, stair climbing, dancing, hiking) provide axial loading that stimulates osteoblast activity through mechanostat signaling. (2) **Resistance/strength training** (free weights, resistance bands, weight machines) improves both BMD and **muscle strength**, reducing fall risk. (3) **Balance and postural exercises** (tai chi, balance training) specifically reduce **fall risk** by 20-40%. Swimming and cycling, while excellent for cardiovascular fitness, are **non-weight-bearing** and have minimal effect on bone density. Patients with severe osteoporosis should avoid **trunk flexion exercises** (sit-ups, toe-touches) due to increased vertebral compression fracture risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NOF; ACSM Position Stand; Cochrane Review 2011)'),
