 NOF; ACSM Position Stand; Cochrane Review 2011)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old postmenopausal woman is found to have a T-score of -3.2 on routine screening DEXA. Before initiating pharmacologic therapy, a workup for secondary causes of osteoporosis is performed. Which of the following laboratory panels is most appropriate for evaluating secondary causes?', '["No laboratory workup is needed before starting bisphosphonates in a postmenopausal woman with a low T-score", "A bone biopsy is required for all patients before initiating any osteoporosis therapy", "Only a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level is needed, as vitamin D deficiency is the sole secondary cause of osteoporosis", "Only a TSH level is required because hyperthyroidism is the only endocrine cause of bone loss", "Complete blood count, comprehensive metabolic panel (calcium, phosphorus, creatinine, alkaline phosphatase, liver function), 25-hydroxyvitamin D, thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), intact PTH, 24-hour urine calcium, and serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) if multiple myeloma is suspected"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Before initiating osteoporosis treatment, a **workup for secondary causes** is essential because treatable conditions account for up to **30% of postmenopausal** and **50-80% of male** osteoporosis. The recommended laboratory panel includes: (1) **CBC** (anemia - myeloma, celiac), (2) **CMP** - serum calcium, phosphorus, creatinine (renal osteodystrophy), alkaline phosphatase (Paget''s, osteomalacia), liver function (hepatic osteodystrophy), (3) **25-hydroxyvitamin D** (deficiency/insufficiency), (4) **TSH** (hyperthyroidism accelerates bone turnover), (5) **Intact PTH** (primary hyperparathyroidism), (6) **24-hour urine calcium** (hypercalciuria, malabsorption), and (7) **SPEP/UPEP** if multiple myeloma is suspected. Additional tests based on clinical suspicion: celiac serologies, cortisol (Cushing''s), testosterone (men), prolactin, tryptase (mastocytosis). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Endocrine Society; AACE/ACE Guidelines)'),
