    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old postmenopausal woman is found to have a T-score of -3.2 on routine screening DEXA. Before initiating pharmacologic therapy, a workup for secondary causes of osteoporosis is performed. Which of the following laboratory panels is most appropriate for evaluating secondary causes?', '["Only a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level is needed, as vitamin D deficiency is the sole secondary cause of osteoporosis", "Only a TSH level is required because hyperthyroidism is the only endocrine cause of bone loss", "No laboratory workup is needed before starting bisphosphonates in a postmenopausal woman with a low T-score", "Complete blood count, comprehensive metabolic panel (calcium, phosphorus, creatinine, alkaline phosphatase, liver function), 25-hydroxyvitamin D, thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), intact PTH, 24-hour urine calcium, and serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) if multiple myeloma is suspected", "A bone biopsy is required for all patients before initiating any osteoporosis therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Before initiating osteoporosis treatment, a **workup for secondary causes** is essential because treatable conditions account for up to **30% of postmenopausal** and **50-80% of male** osteoporosis. The recommended laboratory panel includes: (1) **CBC** (anemia - myeloma, celiac), (2) **CMP** - serum calcium, phosphorus, creatinine (renal osteodystrophy), alkaline phosphatase (Paget''s, osteomalacia), liver function (hepatic osteodystrophy), (3) **25-hydroxyvitamin D** (deficiency/insufficiency), (4) **TSH** (hyperthyroidism accelerates bone turnover), (5) **Intact PTH** (primary hyperparathyroidism), (6) **24-hour urine calcium** (hypercalciuria, malabsorption), and (7) **SPEP/UPEP** if multiple myeloma is suspected. Additional tests based on clinical suspicion: celiac serologies, cortisol (Cushing''s), testosterone (men), prolactin, tryptase (mastocytosis). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Endocrine Society; AACE/ACE Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old woman presents with diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, and a waddling gait. DEXA shows a T-score of -3.0. Laboratory results reveal a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level of 4 ng/mL, serum calcium 7.2 mg/dL, phosphorus 2.3 mg/dL, and elevated alkaline phosphatase. PTH is markedly elevated. Which of the following is the most important distinction and initial management step?', '["The elevated PTH confirms primary hyperparathyroidism; refer for parathyroidectomy before any other intervention", "Start alendronate immediately along with low-dose vitamin D (400 IU/day) because the T-score confirms osteoporosis", "This presentation is consistent with osteomalacia (impaired bone mineralization due to severe vitamin D deficiency) rather than primary osteoporosis; correct the vitamin D deficiency with high-dose ergocalciferol or cholecalciferol before initiating bisphosphonate therapy, as bisphosphonates are contraindicated in hypocalcemia and osteomalacia", "Initiate teriparatide because the PTH is already elevated, indicating the patient will respond well to PTH analog therapy", "No treatment is needed; this is normal age-related bone loss and all laboratory values are expected in older adults"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Osteomalacia** (defective mineralization of newly formed osteoid) must be distinguished from **osteoporosis** (reduced bone mass with normal mineralization). Key features of osteomalacia: (1) **Severe vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D < 10 ng/mL), (2) **Hypocalcemia and hypophosphatemia**, (3) **Elevated alkaline phosphatase** (reflecting osteoblast activity attempting to mineralize deficient osteoid), (4) **Secondary hyperparathyroidism** (elevated PTH driven by hypocalcemia), (5) **Diffuse bone pain, proximal myopathy, and waddling gait**. The low DEXA T-score in osteomalacia reflects undermineralized bone, not necessarily reduced bone volume. **Bisphosphonates are contraindicated** until vitamin D deficiency and hypocalcemia are corrected, as they can worsen hypocalcemia and impair the healing of undermineralized bone. Treatment: high-dose vitamin D repletion (50,000 IU weekly for 8-12 weeks, then maintenance). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Current Geriatrics; Harrison''s Endocrinology)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old woman presents with diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, and a waddling gait. DEXA shows a T-score of -3.0. Laboratory results reveal a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level of 8 ng/mL, serum calcium 7.8 mg/dL, phosphorus 2.0 mg/dL, and elevated alkaline phosphatase. PTH is markedly elevated. Which of the following is the most important distinction and initial management step?', '["This presentation is consistent with osteomalacia (impaired bone mineralization due to severe vitamin D deficiency) rather than primary osteoporosis; correct the vitamin D deficiency with high-dose ergocalciferol or cholecalciferol before initiating bisphosphonate therapy, as bisphosphonates are contraindicated in hypocalcemia and osteomalacia", "No treatment is needed; this is normal age-related bone loss and all laboratory values are expected in older adults", "Start alendronate immediately along with low-dose vitamin D (400 IU/day) because the T-score confirms osteoporosis", "The elevated PTH confirms primary hyperparathyroidism; refer for parathyroidectomy before any other intervention", "Initiate teriparatide because the PTH is already elevated, indicating the patient will respond well to PTH analog therapy"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Osteomalacia** (defective mineralization of newly formed osteoid) must be distinguished from **osteoporosis** (reduced bone mass with normal mineralization). Key features of osteomalacia: (1) **Severe vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D < 10 ng/mL), (2) **Hypocalcemia and hypophosphatemia**, (3) **Elevated alkaline phosphatase** (reflecting osteoblast activity attempting to mineralize deficient osteoid), (4) **Secondary hyperparathyroidism** (elevated PTH driven by hypocalcemia), (5) **Diffuse bone pain, proximal myopathy, and waddling gait**. The low DEXA T-score in osteomalacia reflects undermineralized bone, not necessarily reduced bone volume. **Bisphosphonates are contraindicated** until vitamin D deficiency and hypocalcemia are corrected, as they can worsen hypocalcemia and impair the healing of undermineralized bone. Treatment: high-dose vitamin D repletion (50,000 IU weekly for 8-12 weeks, then maintenance). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Current Geriatrics; Harrison''s Endocrinology)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old female presents with diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, and a waddling gait. DEXA shows a T-score of -3.0. Laboratory results reveal a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level of 6 ng/mL, serum calcium 7.6 mg/dL, phosphorus 2.4 mg/dL, and elevated alkaline phosphatase. PTH is markedly elevated. Which of the following is the most important distinction and initial management step?', '["The elevated PTH confirms primary hyperparathyroidism; refer for parathyroidectomy before any other intervention", "Start alendronate immediately along with low-dose vitamin D (400 IU/day) because the T-score confirms osteoporosis", "No treatment is needed; this is normal age-related bone loss and all laboratory values are expected in older adults", "This presentation is consistent with osteomalacia (impaired bone mineralization due to severe vitamin D deficiency) rather than primary osteoporosis; correct the vitamin D deficiency with high-dose ergocalciferol or cholecalciferol before initiating bisphosphonate therapy, as bisphosphonates are contraindicated in hypocalcemia and osteomalacia", "Initiate teriparatide because the PTH is already elevated, indicating the patient will respond well to PTH analog therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Osteomalacia** (defective mineralization of newly formed osteoid) must be distinguished from **osteoporosis** (reduced bone mass with normal mineralization). Key features of osteomalacia: (1) **Severe vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D < 10 ng/mL), (2) **Hypocalcemia and hypophosphatemia**, (3) **Elevated alkaline phosphatase** (reflecting osteoblast activity attempting to mineralize deficient osteoid), (4) **Secondary hyperparathyroidism** (elevated PTH driven by hypocalcemia), (5) **Diffuse bone pain, proximal myopathy, and waddling gait**. The low DEXA T-score in osteomalacia reflects undermineralized bone, not necessarily reduced bone volume. **Bisphosphonates are contraindicated** until vitamin D deficiency and hypocalcemia are corrected, as they can worsen hypocalcemia and impair the healing of undermineralized bone. Treatment: high-dose vitamin D repletion (50,000 IU weekly for 8-12 weeks, then maintenance). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Current Geriatrics; Harrison''s Endocrinology)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old woman presents with diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, and a waddling gait. DEXA shows a T-score of -3.0. Laboratory results reveal a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level of 5 ng/mL, serum calcium 7.5 mg/dL, phosphorus 2.3 mg/dL, and elevated alkaline phosphatase. PTH is markedly elevated. Which of the following is the most important distinction and initial management step?', '["This presentation is consistent with osteomalacia (impaired bone mineralization due to severe vitamin D deficiency) rather than primary osteoporosis; correct the vitamin D deficiency with high-dose ergocalciferol or cholecalciferol before initiating bisphosphonate therapy, as bisphosphonates are contraindicated in hypocalcemia and osteomalacia", "Initiate teriparatide because the PTH is already elevated, indicating the patient will respond well to PTH analog therapy", "No treatment is needed; this is normal age-related bone loss and all laboratory values are expected in older adults", "The elevated PTH confirms primary hyperparathyroidism; refer for parathyroidectomy before any other intervention", "Start alendronate immediately along with low-dose vitamin D (400 IU/day) because the T-score confirms osteoporosis"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Osteomalacia** (defective mineralization of newly formed osteoid) must be distinguished from **osteoporosis** (reduced bone mass with normal mineralization). Key features of osteomalacia: (1) **Severe vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D < 10 ng/mL), (2) **Hypocalcemia and hypophosphatemia**, (3) **Elevated alkaline phosphatase** (reflecting osteoblast activity attempting to mineralize deficient osteoid), (4) **Secondary hyperparathyroidism** (elevated PTH driven by hypocalcemia), (5) **Diffuse bone pain, proximal myopathy, and waddling gait**. The low DEXA T-score in osteomalacia reflects undermineralized bone, not necessarily reduced bone volume. **Bisphosphonates are contraindicated** until vitamin D deficiency and hypocalcemia are correc