    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old female presents with diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, and a waddling gait. DEXA shows a T-score of -3.0. Laboratory results reveal a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level of 5 ng/mL, serum calcium 7.8 mg/dL, phosphorus 2.2 mg/dL, and elevated alkaline phosphatase. PTH is markedly elevated. Which of the following is the most important distinction and initial management step?', '["This presentation is consistent with osteomalacia (impaired bone mineralization due to severe vitamin D deficiency) rather than primary osteoporosis; correct the vitamin D deficiency with high-dose ergocalciferol or cholecalciferol before initiating bisphosphonate therapy, as bisphosphonates are contraindicated in hypocalcemia and osteomalacia", "No treatment is needed; this is normal age-related bone loss and all laboratory values are expected in older adults", "Initiate teriparatide because the PTH is already elevated, indicating the patient will respond well to PTH analog therapy", "Start alendronate immediately along with low-dose vitamin D (400 IU/day) because the T-score confirms osteoporosis", "The elevated PTH confirms primary hyperparathyroidism; refer for parathyroidectomy before any other intervention"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Osteomalacia** (defective mineralization of newly formed osteoid) must be distinguished from **osteoporosis** (reduced bone mass with normal mineralization). Key features of osteomalacia: (1) **Severe vitamin D deficiency** (25(OH)D < 10 ng/mL), (2) **Hypocalcemia and hypophosphatemia**, (3) **Elevated alkaline phosphatase** (reflecting osteoblast activity attempting to mineralize deficient osteoid), (4) **Secondary hyperparathyroidism** (elevated PTH driven by hypocalcemia), (5) **Diffuse bone pain, proximal myopathy, and waddling gait**. The low DEXA T-score in osteomalacia reflects undermineralized bone, not necessarily reduced bone volume. **Bisphosphonates are contraindicated** until vitamin D deficiency and hypocalcemia are corrected, as they can worsen hypocalcemia and impair the healing of undermineralized bone. Treatment: high-dose vitamin D repletion (50,000 IU weekly for 8-12 weeks, then maintenance). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Current Geriatrics; Harrison''s Endocrinology)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old female sustains a low-trauma fall and is diagnosed with a femoral neck fracture. She had been living independently prior to the fall. The patient''s family asks about the expected outcomes after hip fracture in older adults. Which of the following statements most accurately describes the prognosis following a hip fracture in the elderly?', '["Hip fractures carry a 1-year mortality rate of approximately 20-30%; only about 40-60% of survivors regain their pre-fracture level of independence, and there is a markedly increased risk of subsequent fractures, institutionalization, and loss of functional independence", "Hip fractures in the elderly do not require surgical intervention and should be managed conservatively with bed rest alone", "The 1-year mortality rate after hip fracture is < 5% because modern surgical techniques have eliminated all post-operative complications", "Hip fractures only affect mobility and have no impact on overall mortality or long-term functional status", "Hip fractures in the elderly have a very low mortality rate (< 2%) and most patients return to full independence within 2 weeks"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hip fractures in older adults represent a **catastrophic event** with devastating consequences: (1) **1-year mortality: ~20-30%** (higher in men than women, higher with increasing age and comorbidity), (2) **Only 40-60% of survivors regain their pre-fracture level of independence**, (3) **25-50% require long-term institutional care** (skilled nursing facility or assisted living), (4) **Increased risk of subsequent fractures** (the risk of a second hip fracture is 2-10x higher than the general population), and (5) Significant morbidity from immobility-related complications (DVT/PE, pneumonia, pressure ulcers, urinary tract infections, delirium, depression). Surgical intervention (hemiarthroplasty, total hip arthroplasty, or internal fixation depending on fracture type and patient factors) is the standard of care. Conservative/non-operative management is reserved for patients who are non-ambulatory or have prohibitive surgical risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Lancet 2010; NEJM 2011; Current Geriatrics)'),
