ve/non-operative management is reserved for patients who are non-ambulatory or have prohibitive surgical risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Lancet 2010; NEJM 2011; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old woman sustains a low-trauma fall and is diagnosed with a femoral neck fracture. She had been living independently prior to the fall. The patient''s family asks about the expected outcomes after hip fracture in older adults. Which of the following statements most accurately describes the prognosis following a hip fracture in the elderly?', '["Hip fractures carry a 1-year mortality rate of approximately 20-30%; only about 40-60% of survivors regain their pre-fracture level of independence, and there is a markedly increased risk of subsequent fractures, institutionalization, and loss of functional independence", "The 1-year mortality rate after hip fracture is < 5% because modern surgical techniques have eliminated all post-operative complications", "Hip fractures only affect mobility and have no impact on overall mortality or long-term functional status", "Hip fractures in the elderly do not require surgical intervention and should be managed conservatively with bed rest alone", "Hip fractures in the elderly have a very low mortality rate (< 2%) and most patients return to full independence within 2 weeks"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hip fractures in older adults represent a **catastrophic event** with devastating consequences: (1) **1-year mortality: ~20-30%** (higher in men than women, higher with increasing age and comorbidity), (2) **Only 40-60% of survivors regain their pre-fracture level of independence**, (3) **25-50% require long-term institutional care** (skilled nursing facility or assisted living), (4) **Increased risk of subsequent fractures** (the risk of a second hip fracture is 2-10x higher than the general population), and (5) Significant morbidity from immobility-related complications (DVT/PE, pneumonia, pressure ulcers, urinary tract infections, delirium, depression). Surgical intervention (hemiarthroplasty, total hip arthroplasty, or internal fixation depending on fracture type and patient factors) is the standard of care. Conservative/non-operative management is reserved for patients who are non-ambulatory or have prohibitive surgical risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Lancet 2010; NEJM 2011; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old woman presents after a fall with a non-displaced femoral neck fracture (Garden I-II). Radiographs confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following is the most appropriate surgical management for this fracture type?', '["Intramedullary nail fixation; this is the preferred treatment for intertrochanteric and subtrochanteric fractures, not femoral neck fractures", "Non-operative management with strict bed rest for 12 weeks; this approach carries unacceptable morbidity and mortality in elderly patients", "Internal fixation with percutaneous cannulated screws or a sliding hip screw; non-displaced femoral neck fractures (Garden I-II) are treated with internal fixation because the blood supply to the femoral head is likely intact, preserving the native hip joint", "Total hip arthroplasty; this is overly aggressive for a non-displaced fracture where the femoral head blood supply is intact", "External fixation as definitive treatment; this is not a standard approach for femoral neck fractures"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Management of hip fractures depends on fracture location and displacement: (1) **Displaced femoral neck fractures (Garden III-IV)** in elderly patients: **arthroplasty** (hemiarthroplasty or THA) is preferred because displaced fractures disrupt the blood supply to the femoral head, resulting in high rates of AVN (15-33%) and nonunion (10-30%) with internal fixation. THA is preferred for active patients with a long life expectancy. (2) **Non-displaced femoral neck fractures (Garden I-II)**: **internal fixation** (cannulated screws or sliding hip screw) is preferred because the femoral head blood supply is likely intact. (3) **Intertrochanteric fractures**: **ORIF with a sliding hip screw (DHS) or cephalomedullary nail** is standard because the blood supply is intact (extracapsular fracture), and arthroplasty is not needed. All hip fracture patients should receive DVT prophylaxis, early mobilization, and osteoporosis assessment/treatment. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; AAOS Guidelines; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old woman presents after a fall with a displaced femoral neck fracture (Garden III-IV) in an active, ambulatory patient. Radiographs confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following is the most appropriate surgical management for this fracture type?', '["Non-operative management with traction and bed rest; this approach carries unacceptable rates of DVT, pneumonia, pressure ulcers, and mortality", "Intramedullary nail fixation; this is the standard treatment for intertrochanteric fractures, not femoral neck fractures", "External fixation as definitive treatment; this is not standard for hip fractures and is reserved for damage control in polytrauma", "Hemiarthroplasty or total hip arthroplasty (THA); displaced femoral neck fractures in elderly patients are treated with arthroplasty rather than internal fixation due to high rates of avascular necrosis (AVN) and nonunion with fixation; THA is preferred for active, high-demand patients", "Closed reduction and percutaneous cannulated screw fixation; this is appropriate for non-displaced femoral neck fractures, not displaced fractures in elderly patients"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Management of hip fractures depends on fracture location and displacement: (1) **Displaced femoral neck fractures (Garden III-IV)** in elderly patients: **arthroplasty** (hemiarthroplasty or THA) is preferred because displaced fractures disrupt the blood supply to the femoral head, resulting in high rates of AVN (15-33%) and nonunion (10-30%) with internal fixation. THA is preferred for active patients with a long life expectancy. (2) **Non-displaced femoral neck fractures (Garden I-II)**: **internal fixation** (cannulated screws or sliding hip screw) is preferred because the femoral head blood supply is likely intact. (3) **Intertrochanteric fractures**: **ORIF with a sliding hip screw (DHS) or cephalomedullary nail** is standard because the blood supply is intact (extracapsular fracture), and arthroplasty is not needed. All hip fracture patients should receive DVT prophylaxis, early mobilization, and osteoporosis assessment/treatment. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; AAOS Guidelines; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old woman presents after a fall with a stable intertrochanteric hip fracture. Radiographs confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following is the most appropriate surgical management for this fracture type?', '["Percutaneous cannulated screw fixation alone; this provides insufficient stability for intertrochanteric fractures", "Open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF) with a sliding hip screw (dynamic hip screw/DHS) or cephalomedullary nail; intertrochanteric fractures have an intact blood supply to the femoral head, so internal fixation (not arthroplasty) is the standard treatment", "Total hip arthroplasty; arthroplasty is the standard for displaced femoral neck fractures, not intertrochanteric fractures, because the blood supply is intact in intertrochanteric fractures", "External fixation as definitive treatment; this is not standard for intertrochanteric hip fractures", "Non-operative management with traction; this is associated with prohibitive morbidity and mortality in elderly patients"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Management of hip fractures depends on fracture location and displacement: (1) **Displaced femoral neck fractures (Garden III-IV)** in elderly patients: **arthroplasty** (hemiarthroplasty or THA) is preferred because displaced fractures disrupt the blood supply to the femoral head, resulting in high rates of AVN (15-33%) and nonunion (10-30%) with internal fixation. THA is preferred for active patients with a long life expectancy. (2) **Non-displaced femoral neck fractures (Garden I-II)**: **internal fixation** (cannulated screws or sliding hip screw) is preferred because the femoral head blood supply is likely intact. (3) **Intertrochanteric fractures**: **ORIF with a sliding hip screw (DHS) or cephalomedullary nail** is standard because the blood supply is intact (extracapsular fracture), and arthroplasty is not needed. All hip fracture patients should receive DVT prophylaxis, early mobilization, and osteoporosis assessment/treatment. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; AAOS Guidelines; Current Geriatrics)'),
