    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old man presents after a fall with a stable intertrochanteric hip fracture. Radiographs confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following is the most appropriate surgical management for this fracture type?', '["Percutaneous cannulated screw fixation alone; this provides insufficient stability for intertrochanteric fractures", "External fixation as definitive treatment; this is not standard for intertrochanteric hip fractures", "Open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF) with a sliding hip screw (dynamic hip screw/DHS) or cephalomedullary nail; intertrochanteric fractures have an intact blood supply to the femoral head, so internal fixation (not arthroplasty) is the standard treatment", "Total hip arthroplasty; arthroplasty is the standard for displaced femoral neck fractures, not intertrochanteric fractures, because the blood supply is intact in intertrochanteric fractures", "Non-operative management with traction; this is associated with prohibitive morbidity and mortality in elderly patients"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Management of hip fractures depends on fracture location and displacement: (1) **Displaced femoral neck fractures (Garden III-IV)** in elderly patients: **arthroplasty** (hemiarthroplasty or THA) is preferred because displaced fractures disrupt the blood supply to the femoral head, resulting in high rates of AVN (15-33%) and nonunion (10-30%) with internal fixation. THA is preferred for active patients with a long life expectancy. (2) **Non-displaced femoral neck fractures (Garden I-II)**: **internal fixation** (cannulated screws or sliding hip screw) is preferred because the femoral head blood supply is likely intact. (3) **Intertrochanteric fractures**: **ORIF with a sliding hip screw (DHS) or cephalomedullary nail** is standard because the blood supply is intact (extracapsular fracture), and arthroplasty is not needed. All hip fracture patients should receive DVT prophylaxis, early mobilization, and osteoporosis assessment/treatment. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; AAOS Guidelines; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with acute onset of severe mid-thoracic back pain after bending down to pick up a bag of groceries. Physical examination reveals point tenderness over the T9 spinous process, kyphotic deformity, and loss of height. She has no neurologic deficits. Radiographs show a 34% anterior wedge compression fracture of T9. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?', '["Immediate kyphoplasty or vertebroplasty is mandatory for all vertebral compression fractures regardless of symptom severity", "Strict bed rest for 6-8 weeks to allow the fracture to heal, as early mobilization will worsen the compression", "No treatment is needed because vertebral compression fractures are incidental findings that do not cause pain or functional impairment", "Emergent surgical spinal fusion and decompression for all vertebral compression fractures", "Conservative management with analgesics (acetaminophen, short-course opioids if needed), early mobilization, bracing if tolerated, calcitonin for fracture-related pain, and initiation of osteoporosis pharmacotherapy; most vertebral compression fractures heal with conservative care"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Vertebral compression fractures (VCFs)** are the most common osteoporotic fractures. Up to **two-thirds are clinically silent** (asymptomatic) and discovered incidentally on imaging. When symptomatic, they present with **acute back pain, loss of height, and progressive kyphosis**. Initial management is **conservative**: (1) **Analgesics** - acetaminophen is first-line; short-course opioids for severe pain; NSAIDs should be used cautiously in the elderly. (2) **Intranasal calcitonin** may provide additional analgesic benefit for acute VCF pain. (3) **Early mobilization** and physical therapy - prolonged bed rest worsens deconditioning and bone loss. (4) **Bracing** may provide comfort. (5) **Initiate osteoporosis pharmacotherapy** if not already on treatment. **Vertebroplasty/kyphoplasty** (percutaneous cement augmentation) is considered for patients with **persistent severe pain unresponsive to 4-6 weeks of conservative therapy**, though evidence of efficacy is debated. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; AAOS Guidelines; NEJM 2009 INVEST Trial)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with acute onset of severe mid-thoracic back pain after lifting a moderately heavy box. Physical examination reveals point tenderness over the T10 spinous process, kyphotic deformity, and loss of height. She has no neurologic deficits. Radiographs show a 34% anterior wedge compression fracture of T10. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?', '["Conservative management with analgesics (acetaminophen, short-course opioids if needed), early mobilization, bracing if tolerated, calcitonin for fracture-related pain, and initiation of osteoporosis pharmacotherapy; most vertebral compression fractures heal with conservative care", "Immediate kyphoplasty or vertebroplasty is mandatory for all vertebral compression fractures regardless of symptom severity", "No treatment is needed because vertebral compression fractures are incidental findings that do not cause pain or functional impairment", "Strict bed rest for 6-8 weeks to allow the fracture to heal, as early mobilization will worsen the compression", "Emergent surgical spinal fusion and decompression for all vertebral compression fractures"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Vertebral compression fractures (VCFs)** are the most common osteoporotic fractures. Up to **two-thirds are clinically silent** (asymptomatic) and discovered incidentally on imaging. When symptomatic, they present with **acute back pain, loss of height, and progressive kyphosis**. Initial management is **conservative**: (1) **Analgesics** - acetaminophen is first-line; short-course opioids for severe pain; NSAIDs should be used cautiously in the elderly. (2) **Intranasal calcitonin** may provide additional analgesic benefit for acute VCF pain. (3) **Early mobilization** and physical therapy - prolonged bed rest worsens deconditioning and bone loss. (4) **Bracing** may provide comfort. (5) **Initiate osteoporosis pharmacotherapy** if not already on treatment. **Vertebroplasty/kyphoplasty** (percutaneous cement augmentation) is considered for patients with **persistent severe pain unresponsive to 4-6 weeks of conservative therapy**, though evidence of efficacy is debated. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; AAOS Guidelines; NEJM 2009 INVEST Trial)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with acute onset of severe mid-thoracic back pain after bending down to pick up a bag of groceries. Physical examination reveals point tenderness over the T9 spinous process, kyphotic deformity, and loss of height. She has no neurologic deficits. Radiographs show a 46% anterior wedge compression fracture of T9. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?', '["No treatment is needed because vertebral compression fractures are incidental findings that do not cause pain or functional impairment", "Emergent surgical spinal fusion and decompression for all vertebral compression fractures", "Strict bed rest for 6-8 weeks to allow the fracture to heal, as early mobilization will worsen the compression", "Immediate kyphoplasty or vertebroplasty is mandatory for all vertebral compression fractures regardless of symptom severity", "Conservative management with analgesics (acetaminophen, short-course opioids if needed), early mobilization, bracing if tolerated, calcitonin for fracture-related pain, and initiation of osteoporosis pharmacotherapy; most vertebral compression fractures heal with conservative care"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Vertebral compression fractures (VCFs)** are the most common osteoporotic fractures. Up to **two-thirds are clinically silent** (asymptomatic) and discovered incidentally on imaging. When symptomatic, they present with **acute back pain, loss of height, and progressive kyphosis**. Initial management is **conservative**: (1) **Analgesics** - acetaminophen is first-line; short-course opioids for severe pain; NSAIDs should be used cautiously in the elderly. (2) **Intranasal calcitonin** may provide additional analgesic benefit for acute VCF pain. (3) **Early mobilization** and physical therapy - prolonged bed rest worsens deconditioning and bone loss. (4) **Bracing** may provide comfort. (5) **Initiate osteoporosis pharmacotherapy** if not already on treatment. **Vertebroplasty/kyphoplasty** (percutaneous cement augmentation) is considered for patients with **persistent severe pain unresponsive to 4-6 weeks of conservative therapy**, though evidence of efficacy is debated. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; AAOS Guidelines; NEJM 2009 INVEST Trial)'),
