    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old postmenopausal female presents with acute onset of severe mid-thoracic back pain after coughing vigorously during a respiratory infection. Physical examination reveals point tenderness over the T12 spinous process, kyphotic deformity, and loss of height. She has no neurologic deficits. Radiographs show a 28% anterior wedge compression fracture of T12. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?', '["Strict bed rest for 6-8 weeks to allow the fracture to heal, as early mobilization will worsen the compression", "Conservative management with analgesics (acetaminophen, short-course opioids if needed), early mobilization, bracing if tolerated, calcitonin for fracture-related pain, and initiation of osteoporosis pharmacotherapy; most vertebral compression fractures heal with conservative care", "Immediate kyphoplasty or vertebroplasty is mandatory for all vertebral compression fractures regardless of symptom severity", "No treatment is needed because vertebral compression fractures are incidental findings that do not cause pain or functional impairment", "Emergent surgical spinal fusion and decompression for all vertebral compression fractures"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Vertebral compression fractures (VCFs)** are the most common osteoporotic fractures. Up to **two-thirds are clinically silent** (asymptomatic) and discovered incidentally on imaging. When symptomatic, they present with **acute back pain, loss of height, and progressive kyphosis**. Initial management is **conservative**: (1) **Analgesics** - acetaminophen is first-line; short-course opioids for severe pain; NSAIDs should be used cautiously in the elderly. (2) **Intranasal calcitonin** may provide additional analgesic benefit for acute VCF pain. (3) **Early mobilization** and physical therapy - prolonged bed rest worsens deconditioning and bone loss. (4) **Bracing** may provide comfort. (5) **Initiate osteoporosis pharmacotherapy** if not already on treatment. **Vertebroplasty/kyphoplasty** (percutaneous cement augmentation) is considered for patients with **persistent severe pain unresponsive to 4-6 weeks of conservative therapy**, though evidence of efficacy is debated. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; AAOS Guidelines; NEJM 2009 INVEST Trial)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old postmenopausal woman presents to the emergency department after falling on an outstretched hand. Radiographs show a distal radius fracture with dorsal angulation and displacement (Colles fracture). She has never had a DEXA scan or been evaluated for osteoporosis. Which of the following is the most important principle regarding fracture liaison and secondary fracture prevention?', '["DEXA scanning should be deferred for at least 2 years after any fracture because the healing process artificially elevates bone density readings", "The patient should be referred for osteoporosis evaluation only if she sustains a second fracture", "Distal radius fractures are traumatic injuries unrelated to bone density and do not warrant osteoporosis evaluation", "A distal radius fracture in a postmenopausal woman after low-trauma fall is a sentinel fragility fracture; she should be evaluated for osteoporosis with DEXA scan, secondary causes workup, and initiated on pharmacotherapy if indicated, as prior fragility fracture doubles the risk of future fractures", "Only hip and vertebral fractures qualify as fragility fractures; wrist fractures are not associated with osteoporosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A **distal radius (Colles) fracture** resulting from a **low-trauma fall** (e.g., fall from standing height) in a postmenopausal woman is a **sentinel fragility fracture** — it should trigger a comprehensive osteoporosis evaluation. Key points: (1) Fragility fractures are defined as fractures from **minimal trauma** (e.g., fall from standing height or less) that would not cause a fracture in healthy bone. (2) Common fragility fracture sites include the **distal radius, vertebral body, proximal humerus, and hip**. (3) A **prior fragility fracture is the strongest independent risk factor** for future fractures, approximately **doubling the risk** of subsequent fractures. (4) **Fracture Liaison Services (FLS)** are systematic programs designed to identify and treat patients presenting with fragility fractures to close the **''secondary prevention gap''** (studies show < 20% of fragility fracture patients receive osteoporosis assessment). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; IOF Capture the Fracture; NOF Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old postmenopausal female presents to the emergency department after falling on an outstretched hand. Radiographs show a distal radius fracture with dorsal angulation and displacement (Colles fracture). She has never had a DEXA scan or been evaluated for osteoporosis. Which of the following is the most important principle regarding fracture liaison and secondary fracture prevention?', '["A distal radius fracture in a postmenopausal woman after low-trauma fall is a sentinel fragility fracture; she should be evaluated for osteoporosis with DEXA scan, secondary causes workup, and initiated on pharmacotherapy if indicated, as prior fragility fracture doubles the risk of future fractures", "The patient should be referred for osteoporosis evaluation only if she sustains a second fracture", "DEXA scanning should be deferred for at least 2 years after any fracture because the healing process artificially elevates bone density readings", "Only hip and vertebral fractures qualify as fragility fractures; wrist fractures are not associated with osteoporosis", "Distal radius fractures are traumatic injuries unrelated to bone density and do not warrant osteoporosis evaluation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A **distal radius (Colles) fracture** resulting from a **low-trauma fall** (e.g., fall from standing height) in a postmenopausal woman is a **sentinel fragility fracture** — it should trigger a comprehensive osteoporosis evaluation. Key points: (1) Fragility fractures are defined as fractures from **minimal trauma** (e.g., fall from standing height or less) that would not cause a fracture in healthy bone. (2) Common fragility fracture sites include the **distal radius, vertebral body, proximal humerus, and hip**. (3) A **prior fragility fracture is the strongest independent risk factor** for future fractures, approximately **doubling the risk** of subsequent fractures. (4) **Fracture Liaison Services (FLS)** are systematic programs designed to identify and treat patients presenting with fragility fractures to close the **''secondary prevention gap''** (studies show < 20% of fragility fracture patients receive osteoporosis assessment). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; IOF Capture the Fracture; NOF Guidelines)'),
