    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old female presents with an acute hip fracture after tripping over a rug at home and falling from standing height. She has never had a DEXA scan. After surgical repair, the orthopedic team asks whether a DEXA scan is needed before starting osteoporosis pharmacotherapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?', '["Treatment should be deferred until a DEXA scan can be performed at least 6 months after the fracture", "A DEXA scan is NOT required before initiating pharmacotherapy after a low-trauma hip or vertebral fracture; these fractures independently confirm the diagnosis of osteoporosis and the need for treatment regardless of DEXA results", "A DEXA scan must be performed before any pharmacotherapy, as treatment cannot be justified without a documented T-score <= -2.5", "Only patients with T-scores <= -3.5 qualify for treatment after a hip fracture", "Pharmacotherapy is not indicated after hip fracture because the fracture was caused by a fall, not by osteoporosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A **low-trauma hip or vertebral fracture** in an adult aged >= 50 is **clinically diagnostic of osteoporosis** and independently qualifies for pharmacologic treatment **regardless of DEXA results**. The NOF, AACE, and Endocrine Society guidelines all state that pharmacotherapy should be initiated after a **fragility fracture of the hip or spine without requiring DEXA confirmation**. In clinical practice, however, a DEXA scan is still recommended (when feasible) for baseline documentation, to guide monitoring, and to calculate FRAX scores. The critical point is that **treatment should NOT be delayed** waiting for DEXA scheduling. The post-fracture period (particularly the first year) represents the highest risk window for **recurrent fractures (the ''imminent fracture risk'')**, making timely treatment initiation essential. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide; AACE/ACE 2020; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old postmenopausal male with a T8 vertebral compression fracture continues to have severe, debilitating pain despite 7 weeks of optimal conservative management including analgesics, bracing, and calcitonin. She is functionally incapacitated and confined to bed. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?', '["Continue conservative management indefinitely because cement augmentation procedures have never been shown to provide any benefit", "Prescribe lifelong bed rest because weight-bearing will cause the fracture to propagate", "Discontinue all pain management and initiate antidepressant therapy because the pain is psychogenic", "Perform emergent open spinal fusion surgery with multilevel instrumentation", "Refer for balloon kyphoplasty or vertebroplasty; these percutaneous cement augmentation procedures are considered for patients with persistent, debilitating vertebral fracture pain refractory to >= 4-6 weeks of conservative therapy, with the goal of pain relief and functional restoration"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Vertebroplasty** (percutaneous injection of polymethylmethacrylate [PMMA] bone cement into the fractured vertebral body) and **balloon kyphoplasty** (inflation of a balloon to restore vertebral height before cement injection) are interventional procedures for **painful osteoporotic VCFs refractory to conservative management**. Indications: (1) **Persistent severe pain** unresponsive to **>= 4-6 weeks** of analgesics, bracing, and physical therapy, (2) Significant **functional impairment** due to pain, (3) **Acute or subacute** fracture (typically < 3-6 months old). Kyphoplasty may partially **restore vertebral body height** and reduce kyphotic deformity. Evidence is mixed (the INVEST and FREE trials showed conflicting results), but these procedures are reasonable for **selected patients with refractory symptoms**. Risks include cement leakage, adjacent-level fracture, and infection. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; FREE Trial, Lancet 2009; AAOS Appropriate Use Criteria)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old postmenopausal female with a T11 vertebral compression fracture continues to have severe, debilitating pain despite 7 weeks of optimal conservative management including analgesics, bracing, and calcitonin. She is functionally incapacitated and confined to bed. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?', '["Perform emergent open spinal fusion surgery with multilevel instrumentation", "Prescribe lifelong bed rest because weight-bearing will cause the fracture to propagate", "Continue conservative management indefinitely because cement augmentation procedures have never been shown to provide any benefit", "Refer for balloon kyphoplasty or vertebroplasty; these percutaneous cement augmentation procedures are considered for patients with persistent, debilitating vertebral fracture pain refractory to >= 4-6 weeks of conservative therapy, with the goal of pain relief and functional restoration", "Discontinue all pain management and initiate antidepressant therapy because the pain is psychogenic"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Vertebroplasty** (percutaneous injection of polymethylmethacrylate [PMMA] bone cement into the fractured vertebral body) and **balloon kyphoplasty** (inflation of a balloon to restore vertebral height before cement injection) are interventional procedures for **painful osteoporotic VCFs refractory to conservative management**. Indications: (1) **Persistent severe pain** unresponsive to **>= 4-6 weeks** of analgesics, bracing, and physical therapy, (2) Significant **functional impairment** due to pain, (3) **Acute or subacute** fracture (typically < 3-6 months old). Kyphoplasty may partially **restore vertebral body height** and reduce kyphotic deformity. Evidence is mixed (the INVEST and FREE trials showed conflicting results), but these procedures are reasonable for **selected patients with refractory symptoms**. Risks include cement leakage, adjacent-level fracture, and infection. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; FREE Trial, Lancet 2009; AAOS Appropriate Use Criteria)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old postmenopausal female with a T8 vertebral compression fracture continues to have severe, debilitating pain despite 5 weeks of optimal conservative management including analgesics, bracing, and calcitonin. She is functionally incapacitated and confined to bed. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?', '["Perform emergent open spinal fusion surgery with multilevel instrumentation", "Discontinue all pain management and initiate antidepressant therapy because the pain is psychogenic", "Prescribe lifelong bed rest because weight-bearing will cause the fracture to propagate", "Refer for balloon kyphoplasty or vertebroplasty; these percutaneous cement augmentation procedures are considered for patients with persistent, debilitating vertebral fracture pain refractory to >= 4-6 weeks of conservative therapy, with the goal of pain relief and functional restoration", "Continue conservative management indefinitely because cement augmentation procedures have never been shown to provide any benefit"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Vertebroplasty** (percutaneous injection of polymethylmethacrylate [PMMA] bone cement into the fractured vertebral body) and **balloon kyphoplasty** (inflation of a balloon to restore vertebral height before cement injection) are interventional procedures for **painful osteoporotic VCFs refractory to conservative management**. Indications: (1) **Persistent severe pain** unresponsive to **>= 4-6 weeks** of analgesics, bracing, and physical therapy, (2) Significant **functional impairment** due to pain, (3) **Acute or subacute** fracture (typically < 3-6 months old). Kyphoplasty may partially **restore vertebral body height** and reduce kyphotic deformity. Evidence is mixed (the INVEST and FREE trials showed conflicting results), but these procedures are reasonable for **selected patients with refractory symptoms**. Risks include cement leakage, adjacent-level fracture, and infection. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; FREE Trial, Lancet 2009; AAOS Appropriate Use Criteria)'),
