    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old postmenopausal female with a T9 vertebral compression fracture continues to have severe, debilitating pain despite 7 weeks of optimal conservative management including analgesics, bracing, and calcitonin. She is functionally incapacitated and confined to bed. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?', '["Continue conservative management indefinitely because cement augmentation procedures have never been shown to provide any benefit", "Perform emergent open spinal fusion surgery with multilevel instrumentation", "Prescribe lifelong bed rest because weight-bearing will cause the fracture to propagate", "Refer for balloon kyphoplasty or vertebroplasty; these percutaneous cement augmentation procedures are considered for patients with persistent, debilitating vertebral fracture pain refractory to >= 4-6 weeks of conservative therapy, with the goal of pain relief and functional restoration", "Discontinue all pain management and initiate antidepressant therapy because the pain is psychogenic"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Vertebroplasty** (percutaneous injection of polymethylmethacrylate [PMMA] bone cement into the fractured vertebral body) and **balloon kyphoplasty** (inflation of a balloon to restore vertebral height before cement injection) are interventional procedures for **painful osteoporotic VCFs refractory to conservative management**. Indications: (1) **Persistent severe pain** unresponsive to **>= 4-6 weeks** of analgesics, bracing, and physical therapy, (2) Significant **functional impairment** due to pain, (3) **Acute or subacute** fracture (typically < 3-6 months old). Kyphoplasty may partially **restore vertebral body height** and reduce kyphotic deformity. Evidence is mixed (the INVEST and FREE trials showed conflicting results), but these procedures are reasonable for **selected patients with refractory symptoms**. Risks include cement leakage, adjacent-level fracture, and infection. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; FREE Trial, Lancet 2009; AAOS Appropriate Use Criteria)'),
